S4. The exclusion re-run on EVERY fork (f_pec = 0.3, T = 1e4 K, conservative scaling)
====================================================================================================
  fork                        z=2.30      z=2.85      z=3.35      z=3.70  min sigma
  F1 const canonical           42.8s       27.8s       12.5s        6.5s       6.5s
  F2 const alt                 48.0s       31.4s       14.3s        7.6s       7.6s
  F3 a0(z) declining           37.2s       22.7s        9.5s        4.7s       4.7s
  F4 a0(z) rising cH           97.8s       75.0s       40.6s       25.1s      25.1s
  F5 local density            141.5s      109.6s       60.3s       37.9s      37.9s

  SOFTEST exclusion across all forks and redshifts: 4.7 sigma (F3 a0(z) declining)
  [OK] the exclusion survives EVERY a0 fork -- the weakest case anywhere is 4.7 sigma, so the falsification is NOT a footing artifact
  DIRECTION OF THE CORRECTION, honestly: the declining a0(z) fork -- the one the framework
  prefers on the canonical footing -- is the MILDEST, because a0 was SMALLER in the past so
  x = g/a0 was LARGER and the gas was less deep in the modified regime. That helps the
  framework. It does not rescue it. The rising and local-density forks make it WORSE.

====================================================================================================
S5. THE CAVEAT CARL'S QUESTION ACTUALLY EXPOSES, which is bigger than the a0 fork
====================================================================================================
  The forest b decomposition (thermal + Hubble + peculiar) and the f_pec split are
  calibrated on LCDM HYDRO SIMULATIONS run with STANDARD gravity. In a self-consistent MI
  universe the IGM's thermal and density structure would itself differ, so comparing an MI
  velocity amplification against an LCDM-calibrated budget is not fully clean.
  Which way does that cut? Most likely AGAINST the framework: amplified diffuse motions
  would inject extra kinetic energy and raise the IGM temperature, widening b further. But
  that is a plausibility argument, not a calculation, and a self-consistent MI hydro
  simulation does not exist. So the honest status of the 7-43 sigma result is:
   * ROBUST against a0 footing choice (S4, computed);
   * ROBUST against the peculiar/Hubble split (scanned in the previous script);
   * NOT yet robust against full self-consistency, because the comparison budget is
     LCDM-calibrated. Downgrade the claim from 'falsified' to 'STRONGLY DISFAVOURED pending
     a self-consistent hydro treatment' -- and note the likely direction is worse, not better.
  [OK] the LCDM-calibration caveat is recorded and the claim is downgraded accordingly

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. QUESTION 1, ANSWERED NO: the forest measurements cannot have used a local a0. Voigt
     fitting is pure spectroscopy; no acceleration scale enters. The data is clean.
  2. QUESTION 2, ANSWERED YES -- I OMITTED THE a0(z) FORKS, and that was a real lapse
     against the standing both-ways rule. Now run: five forks, four redshifts.
  3. THE RESULT SURVIVES ALL OF THEM. Weakest exclusion anywhere is 4.7 sigma.
     The declining canonical a0(z) fork helps the framework most (a0 smaller in the past ->
     x larger -> less deep) but not nearly enough; rising and local-density forks are worse.
  4. THE REAL SOFT SPOT IS NOT a0 -- it is that the b budget is LCDM-calibrated. The claim
     is downgraded to STRONGLY DISFAVOURED pending a self-consistent MI hydro calculation.
     Good question; it did not overturn the result, but it did correct the method and it
     found the caveat that actually matters.
====================================================================================================
